                <pb facs="0602" n="507" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg507"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13393: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-03-10T16:00:00+08:00">March 10, 1936—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 10—8 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>123. Embassy’s 115, March 6, 4 p.m.<anchor n="62" xml:id="d520-f1"/> The Military
                    Attaché of the Embassy has returned from a short trip to Taiyuanfu and Fenchow.
                    It is his opinion that Fenchow will not be occupied by the Communists, although
                    there are Communist troops inoccupation of villages in the vicinity of Fenchow.
                    According to the report of the military commander at Fenchow, some 27, 000
                    Communist forces have crossed from Shensi into Shansi, but this is believed to
                    be exaggerated. He does not believe that the Communist activity in Shansi has
                    any direct <pb facs="0603" n="508" xml:id="pg508"/> relation to Sino-Japanese
                    affairs. He reports that six Americans (4 women and 2 men) are now at
                    Fenchow.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
